==English==

===Etymology===
From the boundary line on a graph of an aircraft's capabilities, especially those of height and speed. Engineering jargon (of the 1950s) popularized in Tom Wolfe's ''{{w|The Right Stuff (book)|The Right Stuff}}'' (1979).

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb|head=[[push]] the [[envelope]]}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To go [[beyond]] [[established]] [[limit]]s; to [[pioneer]].
#: {{ux|en|They '''pushed the envelope''' on pricing derivatives.}}

